#!/usr/bin/python3
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  auros-bootchain — the only thing on an installed AurOS that writes
#  what starts the computer
#
#    bootchain menu    write /boot/grub/grub.cfg from grub.cfg.in
#    bootchain sync    put Ubuntu's newest signed shim + grub on
#                      AurOS's own EFI partition, if this firmware
#                      will start them AND they will start everything
#                      this computer needs to start
#    bootchain status  say what is installed, what is waiting, and why
#
#  WHY IT EXISTS. Ubuntu's kernel, grub and shim packages update the
#  boot chain with two programs: update-grub (grub-mkconfig) and
#  grub-install. On AurOS both are wrong:
#
#    - update-grub regenerates grub.cfg from /etc/grub.d. AurOS's menu
#      is its own, and the generated one has no "Put Windows back" and
#      no Windows entry: the first kernel security update would take
#      away the way back, silently.
#    - grub-install writes to /boot/efi/EFI/ubuntu, which is not where
#      AurOS's firmware entry points, so new shims and grubs would never
#      be used -- and it adds its own entry at the head of BootOrder,
#      which only `aurfirst confirm` may write.
#
#  So the image diverts both (build/forge, stage 3) to this, and the
#  kernel, grub and shim take security updates like everything else.
#
#  WHAT `sync` CHECKS BEFORE A BYTE IS WRITTEN. A shim the firmware does
#  not trust, or one that refuses what it has to start, is a computer
#  that starts nothing, owned by somebody who has never heard of a shim.
#  Every check below is the firmware's or shim's own rule, done here
#  first, against this machine's own variables:
#
#    1. the firmware will start the shim. One of its signatures must be
#       VALID -- the file's Authenticode digest is the signed one, the
#       signed attributes verify under the signer's key -- and its
#       signer must chain to a certificate in db. If ANY signature
#       chains to dbx, or the file's hash is in dbx, the firmware
#       refuses the whole image (EDK2's DxeImageVerificationLib), so we
#       do too. sbverify cannot be this check: it trusts the
#       certificates a signature carries, and passes a shim against a
#       certificate made up a moment ago. This is the Microsoft
#       2011 -> 2023 change: a PC whose db never got the 2023 key keeps
#       the shim it has.
#    2. the new shim will start everything this machine starts through
#       it: the grub, every kernel in the menu, and the restore kernel
#       the installer left on Windows' EFI partition (which AurOS can
#       never update). Each must chain to the shim's own vendor
#       certificate (or db), be revoked by neither its vendor dbx nor
#       the firmware's dbx, and pass SBAT at the shim's own "latest"
#       level and the level already in NVRAM. The shim WRITES its level
#       into NVRAM the first time it runs, and nothing lowers it again
#       -- so a shim newer than the grub it ships with is a machine that
#       is broken for good, not until the next update.
#    3. the pair goes down in an order whose half-way state starts: grub
#       first if the old shim starts the new grub, shim first if the new
#       shim starts the old grub, nothing at all if neither. Every file
#       is synced to the disk before the next is touched.
#    4. when no new shim will do -- a PC whose db has only the 2011 key,
#       once Canonical's shims are 2023-only -- a new grub still goes in
#       on its own if the shim this PC has starts it. Leaving the grub
#       behind is how an SBAT update later stops AurOS starting.
#
#  A refusal changes nothing on disk and is not an error to apt: the
#  machine keeps the pair it starts with today, and `status` says why.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
